  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-5,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-1,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4p(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-1,2),$ we substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1+5)^{2} & =4p(2+6) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(0+6) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5.$",
